   "key": "6d4db6c18e63",
   "file": "story-040-1.opus",
   "dur": 151.852,
   "chars": 2072,
   "speech": "“And that problem in three moves I fished out back when I used to take a certain little magazine. The moment I hear your voice, I’ll set about solving it — straight off, at full gallop… Well then, sir… I’m sitting there, and suddenly I hear my wife calling me from the porch: ‘Misha!’ And I hear that she’s calling, but answer her, imagine, I cannot — my tongue was fettered, and that was that… Only later did I guess what the trick of it was: my mood at that moment was, so to speak, the most unearthly, a rare mood even, and here I’d have to talk about some household matter, all sorts of trifles. I keep silent. She calls a second time: ‘Mi-isha! Have you fallen asleep, or what?’ Then I lost my temper and said to her — forgive me — these very rude words: ‘Go to the devil!’ Very well, sir. She went away. And I grew so sad after that, I can’t even tell you. I’ll go to bed, I think. I undressed, lay down, but still I can’t sleep; all sorts of circles flicker, glowing flies go running… And my heart, I must tell you, has long been out of order… And so it began to play all sorts of tricks… Now it stops, now it strikes like a drumroll, so hard that there isn’t air enough… Fear took hold of me, I broke into a fever… I’m dying, I think to myself… And as soon as I thought it, the bed floated away beneath me, and I can’t feel myself at all… Well, all right. It passed, I came to… but I can’t sleep any longer… All sorts of thoughts run on, run like dogs in the street, all sorts of images flicker, memories… Then I see the little girl from the morning walking, after her the young lady, then the old woman… that whole procession moves along as if alive… And no sooner, you know, had my thought stopped on that old woman than I began to tremble and shouted at the top of my voice: I feel that one more turn of thought, and I shall understand — you understand — I shall understand and solve the whole riddle of death and life, as plainly as two times two is four… And I feel that the moment I understand it, in that very same moment… I shall die… I shall not bear it.”",
